2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT ≤p style="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px Helvetica;">SIVERT and HANNA HAFSO≤/p>gersund, Norway in 1884, the eldest of twelve children. As a teenager, he worked as a store clerk before apprenticing for a year as a ca
2 CONC rpenter. In 1902, due to tough economic conditions, Sivert, along with two friends (one of whom was Ole Sorenson), left Norway for Canada. They came by ship, docking&nbsp;≤/p>setts in April and made their way West working on farms
2 CONC  in the Dakotas before arriving in Alberta in 1903.≤/p>teading six miles northeast of Viking on S.E. 30-48-12. For several winters he worked at the Fraser Lumber Sawmill in Ed-monton. Within the next few years, three of his younge
2 CONC r brothers, Tollak, Nels and the youngest, Carl, came to Canada and established homesteads further east in the Maple Lodge district.≤/p>e="margin: 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px; font: 12.0px Helvetica;">Sivert Hafso home built in 1919 a
2 CONC nd still in use in 1991 by Brent (grandson) and Dorothy Hafso.≤/p>0px; font: 12.0px Helvetica;">In late 1907, Sivert returned to Egersund for a visit and while there met and became engaged to Hanna Fotland, the daughter of a baker
2 CONC . He returned to Viking alone in 1908 to continue work on the homestead. In 1910, Hanna, together with Sivert's cousin Anna (Hafso) Thomson, who still resides in Viking, left Norway for Canada. They arrived by ship in Montreal and
2 CONC  came West by train.&nbsp;≤/p>ring to town in 1953. Together they raised six daughters and six sons: Amelia (Mol-ly), Harriet, Signe, Anna, Edith and Gladys; John-ny, Herman,≤/p>2.0px Helvetica;">Arnold, Stan, Gordon and Roy,≤/p>e
2 CONC tired in Vik-ing. Their eldest sister, Molly Cooper, has recently moved back to Viking after living many years in Edmonton.≤/p>
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
